//                                (0.5-4, user: "how flat/wide the fairing is"): the fillet is a circle in the
//                                section squeezed across by it, so a quarter ellipse spread x wide to 1 tall,
//                                tangent at both ends, socket and hub alike.
//                                wing 1.066 / mold 2.138: the faired tail's plan edge (each station's foot behind
//                                the TE, the plan radius) is published (socket web.edge, hub.plan, which also
//                                carries the hub's own block past the TE), and the mold's outline - the pinch-off
//                                groove and the cavity's - follows it, with parting stations across the hub (user,
//                                with a picture: the groove did not follow the fairing). The spanwise seam and the
//                                parting check keep clear of a faired socket's whole reach, not only the nacelle.
//                                mold 2.139: loose bodies under 1 mm3 (specks the cavity cuts free, at the tips and
//                                where a faired tail leaves the TE) are deleted before the plugs gather the loose
//                                cores and before the body count (user: four "socket plug" bodies at the TE).
//                                wing 1.067: Pocket floor on the flat hub (user: "a v contour with filleted edges ...
//                                to help with rigidity"): Flat, V groove or V ridge, V depth and V fillet; the
//                                floor falls (rises) from the walls to the centreline, shoulders and apex rounded;
//                                the bolt holes start from the centreline floor, published as floorZ (the walls'
//                                as floorWallZ), so the mold's cones follow. fuselage 5.007: the hub body's tongue
//                                takes the matching V, offset by the clearance along each face (twin hub_v_twin.py).
//                                wing 1.068: Bolts from = Fuselage, nut inserts (Custom hub; user: through holes with
//                                flanged inserts below, a hand fit, solid laminate between the skins carrying the
//                                clamp; the concern twist and yaw): through holes, the insert's bore and the flange's
//                                flush recess from the underside at Insert fit; warns when the insert reaches within
//                                1 mm of the pocket floor; the notice estimates the clamp at full preload and the
//                                twist and yaw it holds. mold 2.140: fills the holes and forms the bore and recess as
//                                bosses on the lower half.
//                                mold 2.141: a wing with winglets failed "No matching function for size(undefined)":
//                                the winglet parting stations carry no aftPts (user's Ultra wing mold).
//                                performance 1.028: the mast's drag summed over 12 strips of the wetted span at each
//                                strip's own chord and thickness, spray at the waterline's thickness (it took one
//                                section at half the submerged depth, so a mast thickened below the waterline read
//                                too little drag; user's 110 kg mast, 0.4 N at 17 km/h).
//                                mold 2.142: the mast mold's third piece forms only the plate's board face (user: "the
//                                top plate should only be for the top of the plate. all features should be on the mold
//                                sides so it releases"): the halves run through the plate to its board face, the
//                                plate's holes and countersinks are filled in their cavity tool, and the lid's straight
//                                pins form the holes to 0.3 mm short of the underside; features open to the board
//                                face (recesses, direct edits; not through to the underside) are left void in the
//                                halves and molded by the lid (user: "and any features on the top of the plate only").
//                                Then (user): the halves form every through feature, the bolt holes' pins and
//                                countersink cones as modelled, the lid only features open to the board face and not
//                                through (the air in the plate's footprint, less the part, holes and through
//                                columns); a release check slides the lid off the board face and the plate-end tiles
//                                down the mast (the user's demold: they slip down from the lid) and apart (MAST_RELEASE).
//                                mold 2.143: the top-face split worked on an upright copy of the plate (box less the
//                                actual plate, as checked by evaluation), turned flat; MAST_RELEASE gives where a lock is.
//                                wing 1.069, stab 4.036: a tabulated profile's built-in polar follows the section's
//                                thickness (builtinPolar, tableThicknessPolar; NeuralFoil fits): until now a thinner
//                                MRC-16 tip read the native 13.9 % section's drag and CL max (user's Ultra, tip 9 %).
//                                wing 1.070: Nose fairing rebuilt as one lofted body (buildNoseFairing; twin nose_twin.py):
//                                a ridge from a nose point to a flat back face on the pocket's front wall, Top shape,
//                                Nose fullness and Crown round, the foot line the plan and the wall section the root
//                                fairing's (which runs the sides only, from the wall), so the sides fair into it (user:
//                                "the sides should fair into the side fairing too"; the 235 hump "needs a lot of work").
//                                fuselage 5.008: with it on, the hub body drops its nose and starts with a flat face on the
//                                wall, the fairing's back face, growing to Body height and Edge round by the hub's back.
//                                mold 2.144: the fairing's volume in the root's solid fill. performance 1.030: the back
//                                face's strips beside a narrower bar counted.
//   build 242                    mold 2.145: Pry sections (user: Easy Composites chamfer the parting line so a wedge can
//                                pry the mold open; "areas not corners ... not the entire length but sections", "no
//                                larger than 100 mm", "only large enough to take a wedge"). On every outer face, 45 deg
//                                V pockets across the parting (Pry length 30, Pry depth 3 per half, Pry spacing 150),
//                                one per stretch between tile seams so each tile gets one and none straddles a joint,
//                                none in the plug / pad channel band or near a corner; parting height by a ray into the
//                                upper half. PRY console line, notice line. Ejector holes are the next build.
//   build 243                    mold 2.146: Ejectors = Off / Dowel holes (wax-filled) / Printed flush plugs, Ejector dia
//                                6, Plug fit 0.15 (user: pins "to hammer complex deep part out of a mold", both
//                                choices in the dialog). Spots from rays: along candidate lines the part's thickness
//                                between the halves' cavity faces, the thickest spot whose rim is smooth on both faces
//                                (no pin, pocket or cone edge); wing / stab either side of the joint nearest the centre,
//                                mast near each end, fuselage one per third. A hole through both halves at each; a
//                                plug's shank is cut from a copy of its tile so its face is the cavity's. EJECT line.
//   build 244                    mold 2.147: Land gap under Pinch-off (user: "set the height of the land to allow glass
//                                or a skin to be laid on top of it"). 0 (default) pinches on the land as before; above 0
//                                a ring from the cavity's outline out to Land width is cut from the lower half, its floor
//                                the groove's lowered curtain moved up by Groove depth less Land gap, so plies carried
//                                out over the land are squeezed to that thickness and the halves bear outside the groove.
//                                At least 0.2 mm under Groove depth. Round a mast's pod the land stays, as the groove does.
//                                Also: printed ejector plugs no longer read as extra tiles or floating keys in the notice.
//   build 245                    mold 2.148: a printed ejector plug's head moves to the cavity side (user: "ejector pins
//                                have to be pushed into the mold, you have a head that would prevent this"; "the head
//                                could be on the inside"). Head 4 mm wider than the shank, 2.5 mm deep, in a counterbore
//                                in the cavity face, its face cut from the cavity; the laminate's pressure seats it on the
//                                shoulder, a punch from outside pushes plug and part inward. Shank flush at the outer face.
export const FOIL_FILE_BUILD = "245";
export const FOIL_WING_VERSION = "1.070";
export const FOIL_STAB_VERSION = "4.036";
export const FOIL_PERF_VERSION = "1.030";
export const FOIL_MAST_VERSION = "2.011";
export const FOIL_FUSELAGE_VERSION = "5.008";
export const FOIL_MOLD_VERSION = "2.148";

annotation { "Feature Type Name" : "Foil wing",
             "Feature Type Description" : "Parametric hydrofoil front wing from area and aspect ratio: NACA 4-digit or tabulated sections (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) with optional polars, superellipse, linear or double-taper chord, sweep, tip drop, washout, tip cap, minimum tip thickness, and an optional tube socket for a round fuselage. The first of six Foil design features; see the linked guide." }
export const foilWing = defineFeature(function(context is Context, id is Id, definition is map)
    precondition
    {
        annotation { "Group Name" : "Section", "Collapsed By Default" : false }
        {
            annotation { "Name" : "Profile",
                         "Description" : "Section family. NACA 4-digit is fully parametric (camber, camber position, thickness) - use it for anything except matching a known published section. Named sections are digitised tables, thickness-scaled to Root/Tip thick % (Camber % fixed, hidden). Where each fits, from builders' results and the polars: MRC-16 (Drela, 13.9 %, Decavitator hydrofoil): anything pumped, carved or taken off slowly; the all-rounder, gentle stall, most cavitation margin in a hard turn. MH 18 (Hepperle, 11.2 %, pylon racing): fast flat-water wings at low lift; cheapest at cruise, tops out near CL 1.1, cavitates first in a turn. H105 (Speer, 12.6 %, sailing foils): slow high-lift take-off, a bigger stab for its nose-down moment (NeuralFoil polar). Clark Y: flat-bottomed home builds; most lift, biggest moment. E817/E818: widest cavitation-free band at speed. NACA 63-412: laminar bucket on a clean surface." }
            definition.profile is FoilProfile;

            if (definition.profile == FoilProfile.NACA4)
            {
                annotation { "Name" : "Camber %",
                             "Description" : "Maximum camber as % of chord. Limits 0-9 %. 0 = symmetric. Typical front wing: 3-4 % pump/SUP/surf (early lift), 2-3 % wind/eFoil, 1.5-2.5 % kite/high speed. More camber = more lift at low speed but more drag and a bigger nose-down pitching moment. Only shown for NACA 4-digit - the tabulated profiles carry their own fixed camber." }
                isReal(definition.camber, { (unitless) : [0, 3, 9] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Camber pos %",
                             "Description" : "Position of maximum camber as % of chord from the leading edge. Limits 10-90 %. Typical 30-50 %, 40 % is the classic NACA choice. Further forward = gentler stall; further aft = more aft loading and a stronger nose-down moment. Ignored when camber is 0. Only shown for NACA 4-digit." }
                isReal(definition.camberPos, { (unitless) : [10, 40, 90] } as RealBoundSpec);
            }

            annotation { "Name" : "Root thick %",
                         "Description" : "Root section thickness as % of chord. Limits 4-25 %. Typical front wing 11-14 %, high-speed/kite 8-10 %. Thicker = stiffer, more room for spar caps and a more forgiving stall; thinner = less drag and later cavitation." }
            isReal(definition.rootThickness, { (unitless) : [4, 12, 25] } as RealBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Tip thick %",
                         "Description" : "Tip section thickness as % of chord. Limits 4-25 %. Typical 8-11 %, usually 1-3 points thinner than the root. Blend between root and tip is set by the thickness exponent." }
            isReal(definition.tipThickness, { (unitless) : [4, 10, 25] } as RealBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Tip profile",
                         "Description" : "Section at the tip. Same as root: one section along the whole span, as before. Any other: the root section changes into this one from Blend from % outward, point by point along the section, so a cambered root can run out to a flatter, thinner-nosed tip the way production wings do. Foil performance uses it: the zero-lift angle varies along the span (aerodynamic twist, so the lift distribution and trim move), the stall check compares each station against ITS section's CL max, and the drag bucket is blended by area. Foil mold follows the blend. With Section data = Built-in the tip gets its own fitted polar; with Polar summary the root's numbers are used for both (one set of 13)." }
            definition.tipProfile is TipProfile;

            if (definition.tipProfile != TipProfile.SAME)
            {
                if (definition.tipProfile == TipProfile.NACA4)
                {
                    annotation { "Name" : "Tip camber %",
                                 "Description" : "Maximum camber of the tip section as % of chord. Limits -6 to 8. Negative = inverted. Typical 1-2.5 on a front wing whose root has 3-4, so the tip stalls later and flies flatter; 0 for a symmetric tip." }
                    isReal(definition.tipCamber, { (unitless) : [-6, 2, 8] } as RealBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Tip camb pos %",
                                 "Description" : "Position of the tip section's maximum camber, % of chord from the leading edge. Limits 10-90. Typical 35-45." }
                    isReal(definition.tipCamberPos, { (unitless) : [10, 40, 90] } as RealBoundSpec);
                }

                annotation { "Name" : "Blend from %",
                             "Description" : "Limits 0-95, per half span. Out to here the section is the root's; from here it blends smoothly into the tip section, reaching it at the tip. 0 = a straight blend across the whole span. Typical 30-60: keep the root section where the chord and the bending are, change it over the outer half." }
                isReal(definition.blendFrom, { (unitless) : [0, 40, 95] } as RealBoundSpec);
            }

            annotation { "Name" : "TE thickness",
                         "Description" : "Limits 0.1-5 mm, and at most 3 % of the root chord. Held constant along the span, then thinned automatically near the tip so it never exceeds 3 % of the local chord. Typical 0.6-1.0 mm for wet-laid carbon in printed molds (room for 2-3 plies to wrap the edge), 0.3-0.5 mm for prepreg in machined molds. Thicker = tougher edge but more base drag and a risk of humming (vortex shedding)." }
            isLength(definition.teThickness, { (millimeter) : [0.1, 0.8, 5] } as LengthBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Points/surface",
                         "Description" : "Limits 10-120. Typical 30-50. Cosine-spaced, so the leading edge is well resolved even at low counts. Above about 60 slows regeneration for no visible gain." }
            isInteger(definition.pointsPerSurface, { (unitless) : [10, 40, 120] } as IntegerBoundSpec);
        }

        annotation { "Group Name" : "Planform", "Collapsed By Default" : false }
        {
            annotation { "Name" : "Planform input",
                         "Description" : "Area + aspect ratio (recommended): enter the area everyone quotes and the shape; span and root chord are solved. Span + root chord: enter the geometry directly (e.g. to copy an existing foil); area becomes an output." }
            definition.planformMode is PlanformMode;

            if (definition.planformMode == PlanformMode.AREA_AR)
            {
                annotation { "Name" : "Area cm^2",
                             "Description" : "Target wing area in cm^2. Limits 100-5000 cm^2. Typical front wing: 1500-2500 SUP/surf/beginner eFoil, 1000-1600 intermediate eFoil/wind, 900-1500 pump/downwind (high aspect), 300-700 kite race." }
                isReal(definition.targetArea, { (unitless) : [100, 1100, 5000] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Aspect ratio",
                             "Description" : "Limits 2-20, projected span^2 / projected area. Typical front wing: 4-6 surf/beginner, 6-9 all-round/eFoil/wind, 9-14+ pump/downwind, 8-12 kite race. Higher = less induced drag and better glide, but less roll agility and more root bending." }
                isReal(definition.aspectRatio, { (unitless) : [2, 9, 20] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Area definition",
                             "Description" : "Which area the target refers to. Projected (top view) is what sets lift and what most brands quote. Developed is measured along the curved span line and is larger with anhedral or gull. Both are reported." }
                definition.areaDefinition is AreaDefinition;
            }
            else
            {
                annotation { "Name" : "Span",
                             "Description" : "Limits 100-3000 mm, projected tip to tip (top view). Typical front wing: 700-950 mm SUP/surf/eFoil, 900-1300 mm pump/downwind, 700-1000 mm wind, 600-900 mm kite race." }
                isLength(definition.span, { (millimeter) : [100, 800, 3000] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Root chord",
                             "Description" : "Limits 20-600 mm. Typical front wing 140-250 mm (low/medium aspect) or 90-140 mm (high aspect, kite)." }
                isLength(definition.rootChord, { (millimeter) : [20, 180, 600] } as LengthBoundSpec);
            }

            annotation { "Name" : "Chord distribution",
                         "Description" : "Superellipse: c = c0 (1 - (s 2y/b)^n)^(1/n), cut off at the end chord ratio, giving a rounded tip. Linear: straight taper to a square tip. Double taper: a steep taper from the root to Break %, then a gentler one to the tip - a wide root flaring into a slim outer wing (the full-base stab, or a high-aspect wing with a broad centre); Blend % rounds the corner between the two. Linear and double taper end in a square tip: Tip cap = Rounded + plan rounds it in plan view too." }
            definition.chordShape is ChordShape;

            if (definition.chordShape == ChordShape.SUPERELLIPSE)
            {
                annotation { "Name" : "Exponent n",
                             "Description" : "Limits 1.2-8. 2 = elliptic (lowest induced drag for an untwisted wing). 2.5-4 = fuller, squarer tips as on many surf/eFoil wings. Below 2 = pointier, glider-like tips. Above 5 approaches a rectangle with rounded corners. Typical 2-3 for most wings, 3-4 for surf/eFoil." }
                isReal(definition.superN, { (unitless) : [1.2, 3, 8] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "End ratio",
                             "Description" : "Limits 0.02-0.6. Chord of the small end face where the superellipse is cut off, as a fraction of root chord. The leading and trailing edges follow the curve all the way to this chord, so the tip is rounded in plan view. Typical 0.04-0.08 for a fully rounded tip; 0.15-0.3 gives a curved but blunt tip. The trailing edge is thinned automatically near the tip." }
                isReal(definition.endRatio, { (unitless) : [0.02, 0.05, 0.6] } as RealBoundSpec);
            }
            else
            {
                if (definition.chordShape == ChordShape.DOUBLE)
                {
                    annotation { "Name" : "Break %",
                                 "Description" : "Limits 5-60, per half span. Where the steep root taper ends and the gentle outer taper begins, as a percentage of the half span. Typical 20-30 for a full-base stab, 30-50 for a high-aspect wing with a broad centre section." }
                    isReal(definition.breakPct, { (unitless) : [5, 28, 60] } as RealBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Mid ratio",
                                 "Description" : "Limits 0.2-1.0. Chord at the break as a fraction of root chord. The root taper runs from 1 to this over Break %; the outer taper from this to Tip ratio over the rest. Must be at least Tip ratio. Typical 0.55-0.7 for a full-base stab, 0.7-0.9 for a wing." }
                    isReal(definition.midRatio, { (unitless) : [0.2, 0.62, 1] } as RealBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Blend %",
                                 "Description" : "Limits 0-30, per half span. Rounds the corner where the two tapers meet: the outline follows a smooth curve from Blend % before the break to Blend % after it, tangent to both tapers, instead of a sharp kink. 0 = sharp. Typical 8-15. Clamped so the blend never reaches the root or the tip." }
                    isReal(definition.blendPct, { (unitless) : [0, 10, 30] } as RealBoundSpec);
                }

                annotation { "Name" : "Tip ratio",
                             "Description" : "Limits 0.1-1.0. Tip chord as a fraction of root chord; the tip is square in plan view. Typical 0.3-0.6 for linear taper (about 0.4 gives near-elliptic loading for an untwisted wing; below 0.3 the tips load up and stall first), 0.15-0.3 for double taper, where it is the chord at the end of the outer taper and must not exceed Mid ratio." }
                isReal(definition.tipRatio, { (unitless) : [0.1, 0.4, 1] } as RealBoundSpec);
            }

            annotation { "Name" : "Min tip", "Default" : false,
                         "Description" : "On = the tip chord is widened automatically until the section at the tip cap start is at least Min tip mm thick, by raising End ratio (or Tip ratio) above the value entered. The notice reports the ratio used. Guards a thin, small-chord tip that cannot be laid up; especially useful on a stab linked to a wing, where the link sets area, aspect ratio and thickness but the tip chord is still this feature's own." }
            definition.minTipOn is boolean;

            if (definition.minTipOn)
            {
                annotation { "Name" : "Min tip mm",
                             "Description" : "Minimum section thickness at the tip cap start (the last full section before the cap tapers out). Limits 0.2-5 mm. Typical 1-1.5 mm for a carbon layup: two skin plies plus a little UD; the cap beyond it is tow and resin. Only the tip chord is changed, never the thickness %. From build 239 the cap's end is also held at 0.5 mm or more (Cap end % raised for the thickness only, said in the notice): thinner, the mold's cavity cut can fail on some sections." }
                isLength(definition.minTip, { (millimeter) : [0.2, 1, 5] } as LengthBoundSpec);
            }

            annotation { "Name" : "Stations",
                         "Description" : "Limits 3-30, per half span. Typical 11-15 for a rounded superellipse tip, 5-9 for linear or double taper. Superellipse stations are spaced evenly around the tip curve; the tapers use sine spacing, and double taper adds stations through the break (five across the blend) so the corner is followed. Tip-cap stations are added on top, and with Tip drop or Gull dihedral about 17 through the drop (build 235), so the wing follows its drop law and the mold's parting stays inside the trailing edge. More = slower regeneration." }
            isInteger(definition.stations, { (unitless) : [3, 9, 30] } as IntegerBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Mirror to full span", "Default" : true,
                         "Description" : "On = complete foil. Off = right half only, for faster edits and section inspection. Reported metrics are always full-span values." }
            definition.mirror is boolean;
        }

        annotation { "Group Name" : "Sweep, alignment and anhedral", "Collapsed By Default" : false }
        {
            annotation { "Name" : "Align line %",
                         "Description" : "Limits 0-100 %. The chord line that stays straight (apart from Sweep aft); chord is added around it. Typical 25 for a classic elliptical superellipse (both edges curve), 60-100 for modern pump/downwind and many surf wings (100 = straight trailing edge, leading edge curves back), 0 for a double taper that should keep a straight leading edge with the root flare all on the trailing edge (the full-base stab look). Independent of the incidence pivot. Moving it aft sweeps the quarter-chord line back at the tips, moving the lift centre aft. Plan-view tip rounding (Tip cap = Rounded + plan) is centred on the mid-chord regardless, so both tip corners round." }
            isReal(definition.planformAlign, { (unitless) : [0, 25, 100] } as RealBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Sweep aft",
                         "Description" : "Limits -100 to +400 mm, positive = aft. How far the alignment line sits aft at the tip, relative to the root. 0 = alignment line straight across. Typical 20-80 mm for all-round wings, 80-200 mm for surf/carving wings with a curved leading edge. More sweep = more yaw/pitch stability and forgiveness, less efficiency." }
            isLength(definition.sweepTip, { (millimeter) : [-100, 100, 400] } as LengthBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Sweep exp",
                         "Description" : "Limits 0.5-4. Shape of the sweep line x = offset (2y/b)^m. 1 = straight swept line (kinked at the root), 2-3 = curved 'banana' leading edge that is tangent at the root, below 1 = sweep concentrated near the root, 3-4 = raked tips. Typical 2-3." }
            isReal(definition.sweepExp, { (unitless) : [0.5, 2, 4] } as RealBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Tip drop",
                         "Description" : "Limits -100 to +200 mm, positive = down (anhedral). Vertical drop of the tip pivot below the root pivot (net, including any gull dihedral); negative = tips up. Typical 0-40 mm for all-round wings, 30-80 mm for surf wings. Anhedral loosens roll and helps carving; too much reduces effective span and can create mold undercuts." }
            isLength(definition.tipDrop, { (millimeter) : [-100, 50, 200] } as LengthBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Drop shape",
                         "Description" : "How the drop is spread between Drop from % and Drop to %. Power law: z = -drop u^e with Drop exp, steepening all the way to the tip (2-3 = flat centre, tips dropping late). S-curve: a smoothstep, flat at both ends and steepest in the middle, so the wing levels off again before the tip - the gull front view of many surf wings." }
            definition.dropLaw is DropLaw;

            if (definition.dropLaw == DropLaw.POWER)
            {
            annotation { "Name" : "Drop exp",
                         "Description" : "Limits 1-6. Typical 2-3. Shape of the drop z = -drop (2y/b)^e. 1 = straight anhedral from the root, 2-3 = flat centre with tips falling away, higher = droop only near the tips." }
            isReal(definition.dropExp, { (unitless) : [1, 2, 6] } as RealBoundSpec);
            }

            annotation { "Name" : "Drop from %",
                         "Description" : "Limits 0-70, per half span. Where the drop begins: the span line is flat (or on the gull dihedral line) out to here, then drops by Tip drop over the rest with the Drop exp shape. 0 = the drop starts at the root, the plain law. Typical 0, or 15-30 for a flat centre with a break. A flat centre with a distinct break and a near-straight drop, as on some surf wings (about 20 with Drop exp 1.3), needs this; the winglet gives the tip rise on top of it." }
            isReal(definition.dropFrom, { (unitless) : [0, 0, 70] } as RealBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Drop to %",
                         "Description" : "Limits 30-100, per half span. Where the drop is complete: beyond it the span line runs level at the full Tip drop (until a winglet bends it). Typical 100, or 85-95 for a wing that levels off before its tips." }
            isReal(definition.dropTo, { (unitless) : [30, 100, 100] } as RealBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Gull dihedral",
                         "Description" : "Limits -10 to +20 deg, positive = up. Slope of the span line leaving the root, for gull wings: the wing rises inboard, then the drop term pulls the tips down to the tip drop. Needs a drop exponent above 1 (no effect at 1). Typical 0 (plain anhedral) or 3-8 deg with tip drop 20-60 mm and exponent 2.5-4 for a gull. Creates a V at the root; large values can cause mold undercuts." }
            isAngle(definition.rootDihedral, { (degree) : [-10, 0, 20] } as AngleBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Winglet",
                         "Description" : "Off: the tip follows Tip drop and Gull dihedral only. Up / Down: the outer part of each half span bends through Winglet angle on an arc of Bend radius and then runs straight, with every section drawn square to the bent path, so the winglet keeps its true section at any angle up to 90 deg. Its size is Winglet area: the chord distribution continues along the bend, and the tool solves how much of the half span to bend so that area comes out. Area and aspect ratio stay the developed values of the whole half span, winglet included; the notice adds the winglet height and the projected span. Foil performance credits a winglet as extra effective span for induced drag (0.9 x its height across the pair). Foil mold molds it up to 75 deg; steeper cannot release from a vertical pull." }
            definition.winglet is Winglet;

            if (definition.winglet != Winglet.OFF)
            {
                annotation { "Name" : "Winglet area",
                             "Description" : "Area of each winglet, cm^2, measured along the bend. Limits 1-500. Typical 20-60 for a front wing. The bend starts where the outer chord distribution has this much area left to the tip, so it must fit: the winglet cannot start inside 30 % of the half span." }
                isReal(definition.wingletArea, { (unitless) : [1, 30, 500] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Winglet angle",
                             "Description" : "Limits 10-90 deg from the span plane. 90 = vertical. Typical 45-70 for a foil winglet; small angles are just a stronger tip drop and Tip drop does that with a smooth curve." }
                isAngle(definition.wingletAngle, { (degree) : [10, 60, 90] } as AngleBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Bend radius",
                             "Description" : "Limits 5-300 mm, on the span line. The bend is an arc of this radius through Winglet angle, then straight to the tip. Typical 20-40 mm. If the arc would be longer than the winglet, the radius is reduced so the whole winglet is the bend, and the notice says so." }
                isLength(definition.wingletRadius, { (millimeter) : [5, 30, 300] } as LengthBoundSpec);
            }

            annotation { "Name" : "LE tubercles", "Default" : false,
                         "Description" : "Leading-edge tubercles (as on humpback flippers and on some production wings): the leading edge moves forward and back sinusoidally along the span while the trailing edge stays put, so the chord swells at each peak. Published tests (Miklosovic 2004, Johari 2007) show a softer stall and lift kept beyond it, for a little drag and sometimes a little maximum lift before stall; for a foil that is take-off, pumping and hard turns. GEOMETRY ONLY in the sweep: Foil performance uses the smooth chord distribution and never credits or charges them in its figures; from build 232 it reports what they would do on a separate line (its Tubercles switch). Foil mold follows them exactly. Adds six stations per tubercle pitch, so regeneration slows." }
            definition.bumpOn is boolean;

            if (definition.bumpOn)
            {
                annotation { "Name" : "Tubercle amp",
                             "Description" : "Limits 0.5-15 mm. Peak-to-trough travel of the leading edge. Typical 3-6 on a front wing (about 3-4 % of the local chord). The section is stretched at a peak and compressed at a trough with its thickness in mm unchanged." }
                isLength(definition.bumpAmp, { (millimeter) : [0.5, 4, 15] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Tubercle pitch",
                             "Description" : "Limits 20-300 mm. Spanwise distance from one peak to the next. Typical 50-90 on a front wing, five to eight tubercles per half span. The tubercles fade in and out over half a pitch at each end of their range." }
                isLength(definition.bumpPitch, { (millimeter) : [20, 70, 300] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Tubercles from",
                             "Description" : "Limits 3-90, % of the half span. Where the tubercles start, from the centreline. Some production wings start them near the centre (from about 5), others outboard (from 12-20). Typical 10-20. Not 0: a tubercle starting on the centreline broke the mold's cavity cut (build 164), and a real wing has its mount there anyway." }
                isReal(definition.bumpFrom, { (unitless) : [3, 15, 90] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Tubercles to",
                             "Description" : "Limits 10-100, % of the half span. Where the tubercles end; must be beyond Tubercles from by at least one pitch. Typical 90-95, leaving the tip cap clean." }
                isReal(definition.bumpTo, { (unitless) : [10, 92, 100] } as RealBoundSpec);
            }
        }

        annotation { "Group Name" : "Twist and thickness distribution", "Collapsed By Default" : true }
        {
            annotation { "Name" : "Washout",
                         "Description" : "Limits -5 to +8 deg, relative to the root incidence; positive = tip leading edge down. Typical 0-3 deg. Washout unloads the tips so the root stalls first (gentler stall, better tip-breach behaviour) at a small cost in induced drag. Negative = wash-in (rarely useful)." }
            isAngle(definition.washout, { (degree) : [-5, 0, 8] } as AngleBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Washout exp",
                         "Description" : "Limits 0.5-4. Twist follows washout (2y/b)^w. 1 = linear, 2-3 = twist concentrated towards the tips, below 1 = twist concentrated near the root. Typical 1-2." }
            isReal(definition.washoutExp, { (unitless) : [0.5, 1, 4] } as RealBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Thickness exp",
                         "Description" : "Limits 0.5-4. Thickness % follows root + (tip - root)(2y/b)^p. 1 = linear, 2-3 = keeps the root thickness (and stiffness) further out before thinning, below 1 = thins quickly away from the root. Typical 1-2." }
            isReal(definition.thicknessExp, { (unitless) : [0.5, 1.5, 4] } as RealBoundSpec);
        }

        annotation { "Group Name" : "Incidence", "Collapsed By Default" : false }
        {
            annotation { "Name" : "Incidence",
                         "Description" : "Limits -10 to +10 deg, measured from the fuselage axis (X), positive = leading edge up. Typical front wing +0.5 to +2.5 deg. Aim for a wing-minus-stab difference (decalage) of 1.5-3 deg; the stabiliser feature reports it." }
            isAngle(definition.incidence, { (degree) : [-10, 0, 10] } as AngleBoundSpec);

            annotation { "Name" : "Pivot",
                         "Description" : "Point each section rotates about for incidence and washout, and the line that follows the drop curve. The root pivot sits at the world origin. Quarter chord (recommended) keeps the lift centre fixed as angles change. Plan-view shape is set by the alignment line, not the pivot." }
            definition.pivot is FoilPivot;
        }

        annotation { "Group Name" : "Tip", "Collapsed By Default" : true }
        {
            annotation { "Name" : "Tip cap",
                         "Description" : "Rounded tip: section thickness falls along a quarter-ellipse over the cap length, rounding the tip in front view; plan-view rounding then comes only from the superellipse end ratio. Rounded + plan: the chord also shrinks along the same quarter-ellipse over the cap length, to Cap end % of the chord at the cap start, so a linear or double taper gets a rounded tip in plan view as well (the area solver allows for it). Flat tip: thickness is unchanged to the end face." }
            definition.tipCap is TipCap;

            if (definition.tipCap != TipCap.FLAT)
            {
                annotation { "Name" : "Cap length",
                             "Description" : "Limits 2-80 mm, and at most 30 % of the half span. Typical 10-25 mm. Longer looks smoother but thins more of the working span." }
                isLength(definition.capLength, { (millimeter) : [2, 15, 80] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Cap end %",
                             "Description" : "End-face thickness as % of the tip section thickness. Limits 5-80 %. Typical 15-30 %. Lower = rounder, thinner tip; very low values with a thick trailing edge give a wafer-thin end that is hard to lay up." }
                isReal(definition.capEnd, { (unitless) : [5, 25, 80] } as RealBoundSpec);
            }
        }


        annotation { "Group Name" : "Section polar", "Collapsed By Default" : true }
        {
            annotation { "Name" : "Section data",
                         "Description" : "Built-in: fitted XFoil data, nothing to type in. E817/E818/NACA 63-412/Clark Y/MRC-16/MH 18 use one real XFoil polar each (H105 a NeuralFoil one, no XFoil polar being published), moved to your Root/Tip thick % by a NeuralFoil fit. NACA 4-digit uses the exact zero-lift angle/moment for your Camber %/Camber pos %, plus lift slope, CLmax and drag bucket fitted from real NACA polars at your thickness/camber - coarser than the fixed profiles (a fit across a family, not one measured shape); use Polar summary for higher accuracy at your exact section. Analytic (default): thin-aerofoil model - camber-line angle/moment, fixed lift slope, skin-friction drag, Section CL max from Foil performance. Polar summary: your own XFoil/XFLR5 numbers at your own Reynolds numbers, via the fields below. Run scripts/polar_summary.py (developer skill) on your polar - the tool never invents section data." }
            definition.polarSource is PolarSource;

            if (definition.polarSource == PolarSource.POLAR)
            {
                annotation { "Name" : "Zero-lift",
                             "Description" : "Zero-lift angle of attack from the polar, deg (usually negative for a cambered section). Limits -8 to 4 deg. Replaces the thin-aerofoil estimate. Typical -1 to -3 deg for a few % camber. For the built-in profiles (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) use Section data = Built-in instead of typing these in." }
                isAngle(definition.polarAlpha0, { (degree) : [-8, -2, 4] } as AngleBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Lift slope",
                             "Description" : "Section lift-curve slope from the polar, per radian. Limits 4-7.5. Thin-aerofoil theory gives 2*pi = 6.28. Replaces the fixed analytic value. Typical 5.5-6.5 depending on thickness and Reynolds number. For the built-in profiles (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) use Section data = Built-in instead of typing these in." }
                isReal(definition.polarCla, { (unitless) : [4, 6, 7.5] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Cm c/4",
                             "Description" : "Section pitching moment about the quarter chord from the polar (usually fairly constant with CL below stall). Limits -0.15 to 0.05. Replaces the thin-aerofoil Cm0. Typical -0.03 to -0.11 for a cambered section, near 0 for symmetric. For the built-in profiles (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) use Section data = Built-in instead of typing these in." }
                isReal(definition.polarCm0, { (unitless) : [-0.15, -0.05, 0.05] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Re 1 (M)",
                             "Description" : "First Reynolds number the polar data below is from, in millions. Limits 0.1-5. Values are interpolated (log Re) between Re 1 and Re 2, and held flat outside that range. Typical: near your take-off Reynolds number (about 0.2-0.5 M for a wing, lower for a stab). For the built-in profiles (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) use Section data = Built-in instead of typing these in." }
                isReal(definition.polarRe1, { (unitless) : [0.1, 0.35, 5] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "CLmax @Re1",
                             "Description" : "Maximum section lift coefficient at Re 1. Limits 0.6-1.8. Real CLmax usually drops noticeably below the higher-Re value, which is why a single fixed CL max (the analytic fallback) can be optimistic at take-off. Typical 0.85-1.1 at low Re. For the built-in profiles (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) use Section data = Built-in instead of typing these in." }
                isReal(definition.polarClmax1, { (unitless) : [0.6, 1, 1.8] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Cdmin @Re1",
                             "Description" : "Minimum section drag coefficient at Re 1 (the bottom of the drag bucket). Limits 0.004-0.03. Friction drag rises as Re falls. Typical 0.008-0.018 at low Re. For the built-in profiles (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) use Section data = Built-in instead of typing these in." }
                isReal(definition.polarCdmin1, { (unitless) : [0.004, 0.01, 0.03] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "CL@Cdmin 1",
                             "Description" : "The lift coefficient at which Cdmin occurs at Re 1 - the centre of the drag bucket. Limits -0.3 to 1.0. Typical 0.3-0.7. For the built-in profiles (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) use Section data = Built-in instead of typing these in." }
                isReal(definition.polarClcd1, { (unitless) : [-0.3, 0.3, 1] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "k @Re1",
                             "Description" : "Drag-rise rate at Re 1: Cd = Cdmin + k (CL - CL@Cdmin)^2. Limits 0.005-0.05. For your own polar, read it off two points on the drag bucket away from Cdmin and solve for k, or use polar_summary.py to fit it automatically. Typical 0.005-0.04. For the built-in profiles (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) use Section data = Built-in instead of typing these in." }
                isReal(definition.polarK1, { (unitless) : [0.005, 0.013, 0.05] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Re 2 (M)",
                             "Description" : "Second Reynolds number, in millions. Limits 0.1-5. Must differ from Re 1 for interpolation to do anything. Typical: near your cruise or max-speed Reynolds number. For the built-in profiles (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) use Section data = Built-in instead of typing these in." }
                isReal(definition.polarRe2, { (unitless) : [0.1, 0.9, 5] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "CLmax @Re2",
                             "Description" : "Maximum section lift coefficient at Re 2. Limits 0.6-1.8. Typical 1.0-1.3 at higher Re. For the built-in profiles (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) use Section data = Built-in instead of typing these in." }
                isReal(definition.polarClmax2, { (unitless) : [0.6, 1.2, 1.8] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Cdmin @Re2",
                             "Description" : "Minimum section drag coefficient at Re 2. Limits 0.004-0.03. Typical 0.004-0.008 at higher Re. For the built-in profiles (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) use Section data = Built-in instead of typing these in." }
                isReal(definition.polarCdmin2, { (unitless) : [0.004, 0.007, 0.03] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "CL@Cdmin 2",
                             "Description" : "The lift coefficient at which Cdmin occurs at Re 2. Limits -0.3 to 1.0. Typically close to CL@Cdmin 1, since the drag bucket centre moves less with Re than CLmax does. Typical 0.3-0.4. For the built-in profiles (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) use Section data = Built-in instead of typing these in." }
                isReal(definition.polarClcd2, { (unitless) : [-0.3, 0.3, 1] } as RealBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "k @Re2",
                             "Description" : "Drag-rise rate at Re 2. Limits 0.005-0.05. Typical 0.01-0.035. For the built-in profiles (E817, E818, NACA 63-412, Clark Y, MRC-16, MH 18, H105) use Section data = Built-in instead of typing these in." }
                isReal(definition.polarK2, { (unitless) : [0.005, 0.01, 0.05] } as RealBoundSpec);
            }
        }

        annotation { "Group Name" : "Fuselage joint", "Collapsed By Default" : true }
        {
            annotation { "Name" : "Fuselage joint", "Default" : true,
                         "Description" : "Adds the joint chosen in Joint to the wing root. The two sockets start hidden inside the wing at the root's thickest point, blend smoothly out of the upper and lower surfaces to a nacelle (round for the Round tube socket, a rounded rectangle for the Square socket), and continue behind the trailing edge to a rear face where the tube or bar enters, its axis through the middle of the root thickness (Socket height moves it). The Flat hub and the Flat mount add no nacelle. Turn off for a clean wing (molding, analysis, or a different mount)." }
            definition.socketOn is boolean;

            if (definition.socketOn)
            {
                annotation { "Name" : "Joint", "Default" : "ROUND",
                             "Description" : "How the fuselage attaches. Round tube socket: a bore in a nacelle on the root for a round tube, held by countersunk bolts (the original joint). Square socket: the same nacelle with a rectangular bore for a flat bar or tongue. Flat hub with pocket: what production wings do - a flat-topped hub on the root, a recessed pocket the fuselage's flat nose or a bar drops into, bolts down through the fuselage or (Custom) up from the wing's underside; no nacelle, no plug in the mold, the stiffest joint; pocket and bolts are inputs to match a fuselage you own. Flat mount (bolted through): a home-build flat-bar layout - the wing sits on a flat bar and countersunk bolts go down through it on the centreline; a Mount pad under the root seats it at the modelled Incidence (bolted straight onto a bar a wing takes its underside's own angle)." }
                definition.wingJoint is WingJoint;
            }

            if (definition.socketOn && definition.wingJoint == WingJoint.HUB)
            {
                annotation { "Name" : "Hub", "Default" : "STANDARD",
                             "Description" : "Standard hub: fixed dimensions so one fuselage fits every wing built with it - 40 wide x 92 long, 3 mm join fillet, pocket 24 wide x 5 deep from 20 mm behind the front, open at the square back, three blind holes of the Hub bolt size at 20 mm pitch from 15 mm behind the pocket's front wall, 10 mm deep (build 208, user): the fuselage's bolt count and positions, fixed by the standard, its bolts always down through the fuselage. Only its position changes: Hub from apex and Hub above apex (its fuselage template was drawn with the hub's top 3 mm above the apex: set Hub above apex to 3 to match it). Custom: every dimension below is yours, Bolts from included. More standards will join the list as production pockets are measured." }
                definition.hubStd is HubStandard;

                annotation { "Name" : "Hub from apex",
                             "Description" : "Where the hub starts, measured back from the root's apex (the thickest point of the section). Limits -30 to 100 mm. Typical 0: the hub begins at the apex and runs aft, so it only fills the space behind the highest point of the skin. Negative moves its nose ahead of the apex, where it would stand proud of the profile." }
                isLength(definition.hubFromApex, { (millimeter) : [-30, 0, 100] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Hub above apex",
                             "Description" : "How far the hub's flat top stands above the root profile's apex; negative sinks it into the section, and it may go below the section's centre (build 200). Limits -40 to 20 mm. Typical -1 to 0 for a hub level with or a little below the apex, the least frontal area; +3 with the Standard hub, where its fuselage template puts the top. Positive stands the hub proud of the root profile along its whole length and lifts the fuselage's nose by the same amount. Sunk deep, the top emerges from the skin further aft and the underside follows it down to keep 4 mm of floor under the pocket; the notice warns where the pocket floor would break through the lower skin." }
                isLength(definition.hubHeight, { (millimeter) : [-40, 0, 20] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Hub bolt", "Default" : "M8",
                             "Description" : "The bolts that hold the fuselage or bar to the hub: M5, M6 or M8 (M4 and M10 allowed; Custom takes Hole dia). With the Standard hub this is the one bolt choice you make - the standard fixes three of them at 20 mm pitch, 15 mm behind the pocket's front wall, 10 mm deep, the pattern the fuselage template was drawn with (build 209, user). Bolts from the fuselage: sets the hub's tapping drill (M8 6.8, M6 5.0, M5 4.2 mm) and the fuselage's clearance holes and countersinks. Bolts from the wing's underside (Custom hub): sets the wing's clearance holes (M5 5.4, M6 6.4, M8 8.4 mm) and their countersinks, and the fuselage's tapping drill." }
                definition.hubBolt is BoltSize;

                if (definition.hubStd == HubStandard.CUSTOM)
                {
                    annotation { "Name" : "Hub width",
                                 "Description" : "Width of the flat hub across the span. Limits 16-80 mm. Typical 28-40: the fuselage's tongue plus 4-8 mm of wall each side of the pocket." }
                    isLength(definition.hubWidth, { (millimeter) : [16, 40, 80] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Hub length",
                                 "Description" : "Length of the hub along the chord, from the hub's front (the root's apex + Hub from apex) aft. Limits 30-400 mm. Typical 120-160: production hubs run past the trailing edge as a block, and this one does too, its underside flat at the trailing edge's height." }
                    isLength(definition.hubLength, { (millimeter) : [30, 150, 400] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Hub fillet",
                                 "Description" : "Fillet radius where the hub's sides meet the wing's skin. Limits 0-10 mm. Typical 2-4: takes the sharp corner out of the laminate and the mold. 0 for none; if Onshape refuses the fillet the hub is left sharp and the notice says so." }
                    isLength(definition.hubFillet, { (millimeter) : [0, 3, 10] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Pocket width",
                                 "Description" : "Width of the fuselage's tongue across the span (the pocket is this plus twice the Clearance). Limits 10-60 mm. Typical 20-32; measure your fuselage's nose." }
                    isLength(definition.pocketWidth, { (millimeter) : [10, 24, 60] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Tongue length",
                                 "Description" : "Length of the fuselage's tongue along the chord, published for the Foil fuselage. The pocket itself is open at the hub's square back (build 200: it is part of the fuselage interface), so the tongue is located by the pocket's front wall and its sides and may be shorter or longer than the hub. Limits 30-300 mm. Typical 80-140; measure your fuselage." }
                    isLength(definition.pocketLength, { (millimeter) : [30, 100, 300] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Pocket from",
                                 "Description" : "Where the pocket's front wall is, measured back from the hub's front end; the pocket runs from there out through the hub's square back. Limits 0-200 mm. Typical 10-30." }
                    isLength(definition.pocketFrom, { (millimeter) : [0, 20, 200] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Pocket depth",
                                 "Description" : "Depth of the recess the tongue drops into, below the hub's top. Limits 1-12 mm. Typical 3-5: enough to locate against the stab's side loads. With the top at the apex the floor is inside the section, so the depth is not limited by the hub's own height." }
                    isLength(definition.pocketDepth, { (millimeter) : [1, 5, 12] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Clearance",
                                 "Description" : "Gap between the tongue and the pocket walls, each side. Limits 0-1 mm. Typical 0.1-0.2 for a bolted fit." }
                    isLength(definition.pocketClear, { (millimeter) : [0, 0.15, 1] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Bolts from", "Default" : "FUSELAGE",
                                 "Description" : "Which side the hub's bolts come from (build 235). Fuselage: down through the tongue or the bar into blind holes in the hub, tapped or with inserts. Fuselage, nut inserts: down through the fuselage and right through the wing into flanged nut inserts in the underside (fields below), so the bolts take full preload. Wing underside: up through the wing, a clearance hole (M5 5.4, M6 6.4, M8 8.4 mm) on the centreline at each bolt, from the lower skin to the pocket floor, countersunk 90 deg so the heads sit flush; the bar or the tongue is tapped instead. The notice warns under 2 mm of laminate between a countersink and the pocket floor. Typical nut inserts for a molded fuselage (the strongest clamp), Wing underside for a metal bar in the pocket. Set Foil mold's Core clear so the bolts pass through solid laminate, not foam." }
                    definition.hubBoltSide is HubBoltSide;

                    annotation { "Name" : "Hub bolts",
                                 "Description" : "Bolts on the centreline between the fuselage's tongue (or the bar) and the wing: down through the tongue with Bolts from = Fuselage, up through the wing with Wing underside. Limits 0-4. Typical 2 or 3." }
                    isInteger(definition.hubBolts, { (unitless) : [0, 3, 4] } as IntegerBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Hole dia",
                                 "Description" : "Bolts from the fuselage: diameter of the blind hole in the hub, 0 = the bolt's tapping drill (M6: 5.0 mm), otherwise the outside diameter of the insert you will bond in (8-9 for an M6 brass insert). Bolts from the wing's underside: the through hole, 0 = the Hub bolt's clearance hole (M6 6.4 mm; 6.4 for Custom), otherwise your own. Limits 0-14 mm. Typical 0. The mold forms the hole undersized and tapered; drill to size after cure." }
                    isLength(definition.hubHoleDia, { (millimeter) : [0, 0, 14] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Bolt pitch",
                                 "Description" : "Spacing of the hub bolts along the chord. Limits 15-150 mm. Typical 25-40." }
                    isLength(definition.hubBoltPitch, { (millimeter) : [15, 25, 150] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Bolt 1 from",
                                 "Description" : "Where the first bolt sits, measured back from the pocket's front end. Limits 5-150 mm. Typical 15-30. The rest follow at Bolt pitch. With Bolts from = Wing underside, put them where the root is thick: the notice gives the laminate under each head." }
                    isLength(definition.hubBolt1From, { (millimeter) : [5, 10, 150] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    if (definition.hubBoltSide == HubBoltSide.FUSELAGE)
                    {
                        annotation { "Name" : "Hole depth",
                                     "Description" : "Depth of each blind hole below the pocket floor (Bolts from = Fuselage). Limits 5-40 mm. Typical 10-15, and never through the lower skin: the notice says how much wall is left under each hole." }
                        isLength(definition.hubHoleDepth, { (millimeter) : [5, 12, 40] } as LengthBoundSpec);
                    }

                    if (definition.hubBoltSide == HubBoltSide.NUTS)
                    {
                        annotation { "Name" : "Insert dia",
                                     "Description" : "Bolts from = Fuselage, nut inserts (build 241, user): the bolts come down through the fuselage and right through the wing into flanged nut inserts pushed into the wing's underside, a hand fit. This is the insert's barrel diameter; the bore is this plus Insert fit. Limits 4-20 mm. Typical M5 7, M6 9, M8 11 mm: measure yours. With a nut the bolt can be torqued to full preload, which tapped carbon cannot hold, and the clamp is what carries twist and yaw at the joint." }
                        isLength(definition.hubNutDia, { (millimeter) : [4, 11, 20] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                        annotation { "Name" : "Insert length",
                                     "Description" : "The insert's length under its flange, the barrel's depth into the wing from the underside. Limits 3-30 mm. Typical 10-15 mm for M8. Its top must stay at least 1 mm under the pocket floor so the bolt clamps the laminate between them; the notice warns when it does not, and gives the laminate left above it." }
                        isLength(definition.hubNutLen, { (millimeter) : [3, 12, 30] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                        annotation { "Name" : "Flange dia",
                                     "Description" : "The insert's flange diameter: a recess this size plus Insert fit is cut into the lower skin so the flange sits flush, and the flange spreads the clamp into the skin and the solid laminate between the skins. Limits 6-30 mm. Typical M5 10, M6 13, M8 16 mm. The notice gives the bearing stress under it at the bolt's preload." }
                        isLength(definition.hubNutFlange, { (millimeter) : [6, 16, 30] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                        annotation { "Name" : "Flange thick",
                                     "Description" : "The flange's thickness, the depth of its flush recess in the lower skin. Limits 0.5-5 mm. Typical 1-2 mm." }
                        isLength(definition.hubNutFlangeT, { (millimeter) : [0.5, 1.5, 5] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                        annotation { "Name" : "Insert fit",
                                     "Description" : "Clearance across the bore and the flange recess for a hand fit (the insert is pushed in by hand, then bonded or held by the bolt). Limits 0-1 mm. Typical 0.05-0.15 mm; 0 for a press fit. The mold forms the bore and the recess from its lower half at this size; ream if the print runs tight." }
                        isLength(definition.hubNutFit, { (millimeter) : [0, 0.1, 1] } as LengthBoundSpec);
                    }
                }

                annotation { "Name" : "Pocket floor", "Default" : "FLAT",
                             "Description" : "The shape of the pocket's floor and of the fuselage tongue on it (build 241, user: a V contour for rigidity). Flat: every earlier build. V groove: the floor falls from the walls to a V on the centreline and the tongue's underside is the same V, so side loads and roll are carried by the V's faces, wedged by the bolts' preload, and it self-centres. The centreline (the bolts) stays at Pocket depth; the floor at the walls rises by V depth, so the laminate under the bolts is unchanged, and the notice warns when the walls are left under 1.5 mm tall. V ridge: the floor rises to a ridge, the tongue grooved to match; the same location, more laminate under the bolts. Both mold from the upper half without undercut. The hub body's tongue follows it at the same Clearance all round." }
                definition.hubFloor is HubFloor;

                if (definition.hubFloor == HubFloor.VGROOVE || definition.hubFloor == HubFloor.VRIDGE)
                {
                    annotation { "Name" : "V depth",
                                 "Description" : "How far the V's apex on the centreline lies below the floor at the walls (V groove) or above it (V ridge). Limits 0.5-10 mm. Typical 2-4 mm on a 24 mm pocket (a 15-20 deg slope each side): steeper locates harder but wedges more under the bolts. Held to Pocket depth less 0.5 mm. A groove raises the floor at the walls by this much and keeps the centreline (the bolts) at Pocket depth; a ridge raises the centreline. The notice warns when either leaves under 1.5 mm: the walls' height for a groove, the depth over the crest for a ridge." }
                    isLength(definition.hubVDepth, { (millimeter) : [0.5, 3, 10] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "V fillet",
                                 "Description" : "Radius on the V's apex and on its two shoulders where it meets the pocket's walls: no sharp inside corner in the laminate or the mold, and no knife edge on the tongue. Limits 0-10 mm. Typical 1-3 mm. The tongue's corners take the radius less the Clearance where the pocket's corner is convex and plus it where it is concave, so the gap is the Clearance all round." }
                    isLength(definition.hubVFillet, { (millimeter) : [0, 2, 10] } as LengthBoundSpec);
                }

                annotation { "Name" : "Nose fairing", "Default" : false,
                             "Description" : "A faired nose on the wing ahead of the pocket (build 241, wing 1.070), as production foils fair the fuselage's front: a ridge on the top skin from a point Fairing length ahead of the pocket's front wall, rising to Nose height above the hub's top at the wall, its feet sweeping into the skin, lofted as one body (no fillets). It takes the hub's full width at the wall and its back face is flat on the wall's plane, so a bar in the pocket or Foil fuselage's hub body butts against it; the hub body then drops its own nose and starts with that face (Foil fuselage 5.008). Its sides run on into the Root fairing (which it needs on) as one surface. Shape it with Top shape and Nose fullness. Unioned with the wing, so Foil mold's upper half molds it; the mold's cut list adds its volume to the root's solid fill. Off (default): the hub's rounded front as before." }
                definition.hubNoseOn is boolean;

                if (definition.hubNoseOn)
                {
                    annotation { "Name" : "Fairing length",
                                 "Description" : "How far ahead of the pocket's front wall the fairing's nose point lies on the skin. Limits 10-150 mm. Typical 40-60: about 2.5 to 3 times Nose height for a gentle ramp; it reaches the hub's full width by the hub's front, so a length under Pocket from + 10 makes a blunt nose. Held 10 % of the root chord behind the leading edge, with a note." }
                    isLength(definition.hubNoseLen, { (millimeter) : [10, 50, 150] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Nose height",
                                 "Description" : "The fairing's height above the hub's flat top at the pocket's front wall, the fuselage's or bar's top there. 0 (default): Foil fuselage's hub body at its default Body height, 22 mm above the hub's underside, so the two meet flush with nothing to type (the notice gives the height it came to). For a bar of your own: its height less Pocket depth (a 25 mm bar in a 6 mm pocket gives 19; a 30 mm bar in a 12 mm pocket 18), so the ridge meets the bar's top level and its back face is the bar's front. Limits 0-40 mm. Typical 8-12 for the hub body, 13-20 for a bar. The hub body errors if it is set lower than this." }
                    isLength(definition.hubNoseHeight, { (millimeter) : [0, 0, 40] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Top shape",
                                 "Description" : "The ridge's cross-section along the nose, a superellipse exponent: 1 a pointed ridge (a tent), 1.5 a soft point (default), 2 round, 4 and more flat-topped. It eases to the bar's flat top over the hub, from 70 % of the way along the nose to the wall. Limits 1-8. Typical 1.2-2. A pointed top sheds water cleanly off the nose; a flat one gives the flattest ramp for a tall bar." }
                    isReal(definition.hubNoseShape, { (unitless) : [1, 1.5, 8] } as RealBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Nose fullness",
                                 "Description" : "The nose's outline in plan, a superellipse exponent on the foot line from the nose point to the hub's full width at the hub's front: 1 a straight taper (pointed), 1.5 an ogive (default), 2 elliptical, 3 blunt. Limits 1-3. Typical 1.3-2. The feet scale with the outline; the crown keeps the wall's share of the width along the nose." }
                    isReal(definition.hubNoseFull, { (unitless) : [1, 1.5, 3] } as RealBoundSpec);

                    annotation { "Name" : "Crown round",
                                 "Description" : "Radius on the two top corners of the fairing's back face at the wall, to match the bar's or the fuselage's edge: 5 (default) is Foil fuselage's default Edge round, which the hub body takes for its first section; 0 for a square bar. Limits 0-10 mm. Typical 3-5 for the hub body, 0-1 for a bar." }
                    isLength(definition.hubNoseRound, { (millimeter) : [0, 5, 10] } as LengthBoundSpec);
                }
            }

            if (definition.socketOn && definition.wingJoint == WingJoint.SQUARE)
            {
                annotation { "Name" : "Bore width",
                             "Description" : "Width across the span of the rectangular bore for a flat-sided fuselage bar. Limits 10-60 mm. Typical 19-25; the default 19 is a home-build plan's extruded aluminium flat bar (19 wide x 25 high), so that bar slides straight in." }
                isLength(definition.boreWidth, { (millimeter) : [10, 19, 60] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Bore height",
                             "Description" : "Height of the rectangular bore. Limits 6-40 mm. Typical 12-25; the default 25 is that bar standing on edge, as the plan uses it, which makes the nacelle 35 mm tall with the 5 mm wall. Lay the bar flat (Bore width 25, Bore height 19) for a lower nacelle if the fuselage is your own." }
                isLength(definition.boreHeight, { (millimeter) : [6, 25, 40] } as LengthBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Bore corner",
                             "Description" : "Radius of the bore's four corners, to match the rounded corners of an extruded aluminium flat bar (user: bar stock is not sharp-cornered). Limits 0-5 mm. Measure the bar and enter no more than its own radius - a bore corner larger than the bar's will not let the bar in, a smaller one only leaves a hair of gap in the corners; Typical 0.5-1.5 mm: 1 mm suits most 19-25 mm flat bar, 0 for a machined tongue. Half the clearance is added to it, as to the sides; the plug's handle and channel in the mold use the same corner." }
                isLength(definition.boreCorner, { (millimeter) : [0, 1, 5] } as LengthBoundSpec);
            }

            if (definition.socketOn && definition.wingJoint == WingJoint.FLAT)
            {
                annotation { "Name" : "Bolts",
                             "Description" : "Number of bolts down through the wing on the centreline, from Bolt 1 from at Bolt pitch. Limits 1-6. Typical 3-4 (a flat-bar home-build plan uses four M6 at 25 mm). Each hole is checked against the root chord and the laminate left under its head, and skipped with a warning when it falls outside the chord." }
                isInteger(definition.flatBolts, { (unitless) : [1, 4, 6] } as IntegerBoundSpec);

                annotation { "Name" : "Bolt", "Default" : "M6",
                             "Description" : "The bolt size: sets the clearance hole (M5 5.4, M6 6.4, M8 8.4 mm) and the countersink's head diameter. Typical M6 for a front wing; Custom takes the hole from Bolt dia and a head twice it." }
                definition.flatBolt is BoltSize;

